    "question": {
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} && R_{3}:=R_{3}+(-1)R_{1} \\\\  & \\sim \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} && R_{4}:=R_{4}+(2)R_{1} \\\\  & \\sim \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $2$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $2$nd and $3$rd columns.",
